1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,960
Hydraulic knees and myoelectric elbows.

2
00:00:05,820 --> 00:00:09,380
Artificial hands that can sense the difference between hot and cold.

3
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:12,220
Prosthetic feet that can feel the floor.

4
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:17,020
Just some of the latest achievements in man's 4,000-year-old quest

5
00:00:17,021 --> 00:00:21,840
to replace missing limbs with devices that function like the real thing.

6
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:26,900
Now, the art and science of prosthetics on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
An expert skydiver prepares for another jump, soars through thin air, and makes a perfect landing.

8
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:55,180
A former high-voltage lineman back at work after a horrific industrial accident

9
00:00:55,181 --> 00:00:57,980
operates a backhoe on his new job.

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,780
A high school baseball coach slides into home, and a child tries out a new bike.

11
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:16,040
Ordinary, everyday activities, except that each of these extraordinary people has lost a limb, and most are double amputees.

12
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:24,640
But thanks to the miracles of space-age materials and design, they are all able to lead active, fulfilling lives.

13
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:29,040
Lives that were an impossible dream just a few decades ago.

14
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:42,860
Man's efforts to replace missing body parts with some sort of prosthesis or artificial device go back to the dawn of history.

15
00:01:42,861 --> 00:01:50,900
From the earliest times, why, if you, to survive, you had to, you had to be able to get food and you had to fight.

16
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:55,420
And if you couldn't do those two things, then what good were you to the tribe?

17
00:01:56,200 --> 00:01:59,460
Force prosthetics was used when man first began.

18
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,220
When a person would have been rolled over by a rock and lost a leg,

19
00:02:06,820 --> 00:02:12,100
the person would then go out and carve out a little leg for themselves out of wood.

20
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:18,020
The ancient Greeks and Romans designed pottery and mosaics

21
00:02:18,021 --> 00:02:22,320
showing the survivors of battles and accidents wearing peg legs.

22
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:27,320
The first written record of an amputation and subsequent prosthesis

23
00:02:27,321 --> 00:02:35,320
The prosthesis is the heroic tale of Queen Vishwa, who lost her leg in battle, only to return to the field on some sort of rod.

24
00:02:35,321 --> 00:02:45,320
The story first appeared in the Rig Veda, the oldest of India's sacred Hindu writings, somewhere between 3500 and 1800 BC.

25
00:02:45,321 --> 00:02:54,320
These were very ancient manuscripts, and mention was made of artificial limbs in those far-off days.

26
00:02:54,321 --> 00:03:01,320
but very little is known of what the actual prostheses were like.

27
00:03:01,321 --> 00:03:05,320
Whatever these ancient prostheses were like,

28
00:03:05,321 --> 00:03:08,320
they had to begin with some kind of socket

29
00:03:08,321 --> 00:03:11,320
that connected the residual limb with the artificial limb.

30
00:03:11,321 --> 00:03:14,320
The definition of a socket is something you put something into.

31
00:03:14,321 --> 00:03:17,320
So it wouldn't be just a branch tied around your leg.

32
00:03:17,321 --> 00:03:19,320
It had to be like a fork of a tree.

33
00:03:19,321 --> 00:03:22,320
Then you put your knee down into it and your legs cut off here,

34
00:03:22,321 --> 00:03:24,880
and your legs cut off here, but you can still bend your knee

35
00:03:24,881 --> 00:03:29,020
and rest your knee onto the fork of the tree.

36
00:03:29,021 --> 00:03:31,760
Eventually, the socket probably evolved

37
00:03:31,761 --> 00:03:36,500
into a wooden or leather bucket, a basic prosthetic concept

38
00:03:36,501 --> 00:03:39,580
that has endured until recently.

39
00:03:39,581 --> 00:03:42,840
Now, that's not surprising when you consider that essentially

40
00:03:42,841 --> 00:03:46,080
the shape of the residual limb that they were fitting

41
00:03:46,081 --> 00:03:50,740
was very similar to the one that we're working with now.

42
00:03:50,741 --> 00:03:53,620
Even the methods of attaching the socket to the body

43
00:03:53,621 --> 00:03:56,500
remained the same for thousands of years.

44
00:03:57,540 --> 00:04:01,720
Sockets of years ago, of centuries ago,

45
00:04:01,721 --> 00:04:04,540
were more often than not attached with straps,

46
00:04:04,541 --> 00:04:08,780
leather straps, and basically by skinning some animal

47
00:04:08,781 --> 00:04:12,160
to make that leather strap to hold the prosthesis on.

48
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:15,960
Prosthetic devices continued to appear

49
00:04:15,961 --> 00:04:18,040
in art and literature.

50
00:04:18,041 --> 00:04:19,660
In the fifth century BC,

51
00:04:19,661 --> 00:04:22,360
Aristophanes wrote a part in his play, The Birds,

52
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:24,880
for a man wearing an artificial leg.

53
00:04:25,940 --> 00:04:29,100
And Herodotus recounted the story of a Persian prophet

54
00:04:29,101 --> 00:04:32,160
named Hegesistratus in The Histories,

55
00:04:32,660 --> 00:04:34,460
written around 430 BC.

56
00:04:35,380 --> 00:04:40,840
The Spartans condemned Hegesistratus to death,

57
00:04:41,660 --> 00:04:44,100
but before the sentence could be carried out,

58
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:47,120
he was apparently shackled.

59
00:04:47,121 --> 00:04:52,620
he is alleged to have amputated his foot and escaped.

60
00:04:54,620 --> 00:04:58,700
A few years later, he is mentioned again.

61
00:04:59,740 --> 00:05:03,440
And there he was with a wooden foot,

62
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:05,460
which apparently he made himself.

63
00:05:06,540 --> 00:05:11,240
And so, Hedges Estratus was his own prosthetist.

64
00:05:11,700 --> 00:05:14,320
Centuries before, there was a word to describe

65
00:05:14,321 --> 00:05:17,000
the people who design and fit artificial limbs.

66
00:05:17,120 --> 00:05:24,720
And he also had to perform his own amputation, which was at best a brutal and bloody business,

67
00:05:24,721 --> 00:05:29,600
since the wounded limb was simply chopped off without benefit of anesthesia.

68
00:05:30,440 --> 00:05:34,960
But even in the pre-Christian period, there was some knowledge of limb preservation.

69
00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:42,240
As early as the 5th century BC, a work attributed to Hippocrates described the use of ligatures

70
00:05:42,241 --> 00:05:45,740
to tie off the blood vessels and stop the bleeding.

71
00:05:47,120 --> 00:05:52,560
But not every early civilization cared about saving the life of an amputee.

72
00:05:52,561 --> 00:05:58,060
In ancient Egypt, for instance, the idea of entering the afterlife with a missing body part

73
00:05:58,061 --> 00:06:02,100
was far more appalling than being disabled in this life.

74
00:06:02,940 --> 00:06:10,340
A dead body had to be buried or put in a sarcophagus, whatever, whole.

75
00:06:11,380 --> 00:06:15,660
And if a person died with a missing limb or part of a limb,

76
00:06:15,661 --> 00:06:20,000
then a funerary prosthesis would be made.

77
00:06:20,660 --> 00:06:24,360
I don't actually know of any records

78
00:06:24,361 --> 00:06:30,560
where Egyptians actually wore artificial limbs in their lifetime.

79
00:06:31,700 --> 00:06:36,300
The oldest known artifact of a usable prosthetic device

80
00:06:36,301 --> 00:06:40,180
was a bronze leg that probably belonged to a Roman soldier

81
00:06:40,181 --> 00:06:43,520
during the Samnite Wars of the 3rd century B.C.

82
00:06:43,521 --> 00:06:51,520
Designed for a below-knee amputee, the leg was excavated from a tomb in Padua, Italy in 1858.

83
00:06:51,521 --> 00:07:01,520
Fortunately, a plaster of Paris replica was made of the prosthesis before it was destroyed during the bombing of London in World War II.

84
00:07:01,521 --> 00:07:12,520
But the iron hand, reportedly used by another Roman centurion, Marcus Sergius, around 210 BC, was never even found.

85
00:07:12,521 --> 00:07:18,600
Designed to hold a shield, the hand would have been both an early example of the armorer's

86
00:07:18,601 --> 00:07:22,157
art and his ability to serve as a prosthetist.

87
00:07:22,158 --> 00:07:25,560
But it was an art that would be lost for the

88
00:07:25,561 --> 00:07:27,417
next millennium.

89
00:07:27,418 --> 00:07:36,120
In times of peace, approximately 74% of all amputations are due to disease.

90
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,880
Accidents account for another 23%.

91
00:07:39,881 --> 00:07:43,880
The remaining 3% are the result of birth defects.

92
00:07:44,840 --> 00:07:48,560
Prosthetics will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

93
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:55,500
Many of the sophisticated surgical procedures developed by the early Greeks and Romans

94
00:07:55,501 --> 00:07:57,840
were lost during the Dark Ages,

95
00:07:58,320 --> 00:08:01,280
which descended on Western Europe around 200 AD

96
00:08:01,281 --> 00:08:04,380
and lasted for nearly 1,000 years.

97
00:08:04,381 --> 00:08:09,700
It was a period of profound piety and great brutality.

98
00:08:10,340 --> 00:08:13,920
Given the battles, diseases and violence of feudal society,

99
00:08:14,620 --> 00:08:17,860
there was probably no shortage of amputations.

100
00:08:18,620 --> 00:08:23,120
But only a few hardy souls could have survived the primitive surgical techniques,

101
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:27,420
which often involved crushing the injured limb before cutting it.

102
00:08:27,421 --> 00:08:33,460
The crushing would seal off the arteries and the nerves

103
00:08:33,461 --> 00:08:39,600
and create a situation where the patient could go through that amputation procedure.

104
00:08:40,260 --> 00:08:43,500
If the patient survived the amputation itself,

105
00:08:44,020 --> 00:08:48,500
he still had to face the agonizing process of trying to seal the wound,

106
00:08:49,320 --> 00:08:51,800
either by pouring boiling oil onto the limb

107
00:08:51,801 --> 00:08:54,760
or by applying some other form of searing heat.

108
00:08:54,761 --> 00:08:58,860
They did a number of things to cauterize the end of the stump.

109
00:08:58,861 --> 00:09:01,260
They'd have a bed of live coals.

110
00:09:01,261 --> 00:09:05,340
They'd take the stump and they'd stick the stump into the live coals in order to burn

111
00:09:05,341 --> 00:09:07,460
the end cauterizing the stump.

112
00:09:07,461 --> 00:09:10,220
They also would have sometimes a bucket of hot tar.

113
00:09:10,221 --> 00:09:18,600
They'd dip the stump in hot tar or they'd heat up usually a sword, broad sword and

114
00:09:18,601 --> 00:09:23,560
they would, after it was red hot, they'd put that against the stump to cauterize the stump.

115
00:09:24,760 --> 00:09:30,000
No record exists of any attempt to provide the poor with prosthetics.

116
00:09:30,001 --> 00:09:35,360
If amputees couldn't create some sort of crude artificial limb for themselves,

117
00:09:35,361 --> 00:09:41,760
they were destined to spend the rest of their lives scooting through filth and muck on wheeled boards.

118
00:09:47,960 --> 00:09:53,640
By the 13th century, the Dark Ages began giving way to the Middle Ages.

119
00:09:53,641 --> 00:09:56,040
Wars raged throughout Europe and Asia,

120
00:09:56,440 --> 00:09:59,960
and wealthy knights, the professional soldiers of their time,

121
00:10:00,460 --> 00:10:02,480
kept their armors fully employed.

122
00:10:03,920 --> 00:10:05,780
Necessity is the mother of invention,

123
00:10:05,781 --> 00:10:11,900
and because quite a few arms were lost in battle, right,

124
00:10:13,180 --> 00:10:14,660
an arm had to be made.

125
00:10:15,200 --> 00:10:16,520
Who best to make it?

126
00:10:16,660 --> 00:10:18,080
The person who made armor.

127
00:10:18,740 --> 00:10:21,640
They were probably the gurus of that time

128
00:10:21,641 --> 00:10:23,000
when it comes to articulation,

129
00:10:23,001 --> 00:10:26,320
because they really were able to do a lot of articulations with the suit of armor.

130
00:10:27,480 --> 00:10:32,060
The prosthetics designed by these skilled artisans weren't always functional,

131
00:10:32,580 --> 00:10:35,480
but they were still essential to the knights who wore them

132
00:10:35,481 --> 00:10:40,740
because they disguised their infirmities and allowed them to return to the field of battle.

133
00:10:41,600 --> 00:10:46,140
They didn't want the shame of their injury to prevent them from participating

134
00:10:46,141 --> 00:10:49,500
in this important role that they had in society.

135
00:10:49,501 --> 00:10:55,100
The design of some of these medieval prosthetics was truly amazing,

136
00:10:55,680 --> 00:10:58,460
both from an aesthetic and mechanical perspective.

137
00:10:58,880 --> 00:11:02,360
There are a lot of perforations or holes cut in this,

138
00:11:02,760 --> 00:11:07,660
and I'm sure that's to keep it light so that the wearer isn't fatigued

139
00:11:07,661 --> 00:11:10,980
by the weight of so much metal on the end of their residual limb.

140
00:11:11,660 --> 00:11:17,120
Among the more remarkable prosthetics produced between the 15th and 17th centuries

141
00:11:17,121 --> 00:11:19,260
is the alt-roupon hand.

142
00:11:19,500 --> 00:11:22,840
believed to have been forged around 1400.

143
00:11:22,880 --> 00:11:26,300
The thumb is rigid, but the fingers move in pairs

144
00:11:26,340 --> 00:11:30,780
and are sprung by the buttons at the base of the palm.

145
00:11:30,820 --> 00:11:34,420
But the most famous of all historical prosthetics

146
00:11:34,440 --> 00:11:37,520
is the iron hand of Goertz von Berlikingen,

147
00:11:37,560 --> 00:11:41,960
a 16th century German knight who remained a German folk hero

148
00:11:41,980 --> 00:11:45,500
well into the 20th century.

149
00:11:45,501 --> 00:11:53,060
He actually said that he could wield a stronger blow with the artificial limb

150
00:11:53,061 --> 00:11:57,400
more than he could have done with his natural limb.

151
00:11:58,020 --> 00:12:03,660
His iron hand was forged in 1509 and is still in working order.

152
00:12:04,320 --> 00:12:08,700
The four jointed fingers are operated either by pushing them against something

153
00:12:08,701 --> 00:12:11,600
or adjusting them with the natural left hand.

154
00:12:11,601 --> 00:12:15,760
By pressing on a button, all the fingers will stretch outward.

155
00:12:16,440 --> 00:12:21,180
The thumb operates in a similar manner and can be outstretched by pushing on another button.

156
00:12:22,100 --> 00:12:27,760
When all the fingers are locked into position, the hand can hold a shield or wield a sword.

157
00:12:29,560 --> 00:12:36,260
All in all, Goertz, who actually owns several of these hands, found his prosthesis quite satisfactory.

158
00:12:36,740 --> 00:12:39,860
With one exception, he is reputed to have said,

159
00:12:39,861 --> 00:12:53,861
My right hand, although very useful in battle, is insensitive to the feelings of love, for you see it is made of iron and is encased within my glove.

160
00:12:55,760 --> 00:13:05,917
In fact, the famous hand of Goetz von Berlikingen, with its remarkable function, was the exception to the rule of iron prosthetics, which were probably removed with

161
00:13:05,918 --> 00:13:09,200
the rest of the knight's armor when he returned home.

162
00:13:09,860 --> 00:13:16,620
They were, in theory, able to articulate and move, but actually, by the time you got through

163
00:13:16,621 --> 00:13:21,060
hammering and rivets and stuff together, it was so hard to do that it was not practical.

164
00:13:21,061 --> 00:13:27,740
And that's an important thing about prosthesis, is that the body only has so much tolerance

165
00:13:27,741 --> 00:13:33,780
for gadgetry.

166
00:13:33,781 --> 00:13:39,040
The greatest contribution to the advance of prosthetics in the 16th century was made not

167
00:13:39,041 --> 00:13:43,480
by an armorer, but by Ambrose Paré, a French surgeon

168
00:13:43,580 --> 00:13:47,040
known as the father of prosthetics.

169
00:13:47,140 --> 00:13:51,660
In 1552, while serving in the army of King Francis I,

170
00:13:51,760 --> 00:13:56,260
Paré reintroduced the use of ligatures in amputation,

171
00:13:56,360 --> 00:14:00,820
thereby sparing his patients the agony of cautery.

172
00:14:00,920 --> 00:14:04,100
Instead of putting his amputation

173
00:14:04,101 --> 00:14:10,480
stumps in boiling oil, he used attar of roses and turpentine.

174
00:14:11,300 --> 00:14:13,580
There's a famous saying attributed to him.

175
00:14:14,080 --> 00:14:19,220
He said, well, I bandaged him, but God healed him.

176
00:14:20,520 --> 00:14:26,320
By reviving a protocol that had first been proposed by Hippocrates a thousand years earlier,

177
00:14:26,920 --> 00:14:29,620
Paré introduced a renaissance in surgery.

178
00:14:29,621 --> 00:14:35,160
But his contributions to prosthetics were not limited to his surgical techniques.

179
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:44,820
He actually designed artificial eyes and noses and various other parts of the body,

180
00:14:45,060 --> 00:14:48,160
which would certainly help psychologically.

181
00:14:48,940 --> 00:14:52,260
The artificial limbs he made, they were quite shapely.

182
00:14:52,261 --> 00:14:56,180
They were made sometimes rather like armor,

183
00:14:56,600 --> 00:15:02,520
with overlapping plates where the joint was across the knee and the front of the ankle,

184
00:15:02,980 --> 00:15:03,840
and they were sturdy.

185
00:15:04,440 --> 00:15:06,360
He also designed an elbow.

186
00:15:06,960 --> 00:15:09,860
It had a spring, I think, about three feet long,

187
00:15:10,600 --> 00:15:13,920
actually in the side of the elbow, and a great big ratchet,

188
00:15:15,200 --> 00:15:18,880
and the arm could be brought up in stages.

189
00:15:18,881 --> 00:15:26,560
The tourniquet introduced by Etienne Morel in the late 17th century greatly increased

190
00:15:26,561 --> 00:15:32,480
the odds of a patient actually surviving amputation surgery long enough to need an artificial limb.

191
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:40,400
And in the 19th century, Napoleon's personal surgeon, Dominique Jean Larre, packed ice and

192
00:15:40,401 --> 00:15:45,920
snow around the residual limbs of amputees to slow the bleeding and dull the pain.

193
00:15:45,921 --> 00:15:53,840
They found themselves where they would fit the prosthesis very, very quickly after the amputation,

194
00:15:54,260 --> 00:15:57,860
because the actual prosthesis would maybe help with the pain,

195
00:15:58,020 --> 00:16:03,560
because it was adding some pressures around that throbbing residual limb.

196
00:16:04,520 --> 00:16:10,420
Despite these advances, the mortality rate for amputees remained appallingly high.

197
00:16:10,421 --> 00:16:20,320
Monsieur Larraie, for instance, is perhaps best known for the number of amputations he performed at one time, 224 hours.

198
00:16:20,321 --> 00:16:25,820
And he was particularly pleased that four of his patients actually survived.

199
00:16:25,821 --> 00:16:31,080
A surgeon's skill was measured in how fast they could amputate a stump,

200
00:16:31,081 --> 00:16:35,680
and that included cutting the meat with a knife and using a saw to go through the bone.

201
00:16:35,681 --> 00:16:39,760
There's a surgeon who could do it in 20 or 30 seconds.

202
00:16:39,761 --> 00:16:46,820
and in one instance where the surgeon not only cut off the person's leg in 30 seconds,

203
00:16:46,880 --> 00:16:51,400
also cut off two fingers of one of the guys holding them down and one of the testicles of the patient.

204
00:16:52,240 --> 00:16:57,080
While war has always accounted for a high percentage of all amputations,

205
00:16:57,081 --> 00:17:01,880
limbs were also lost to disease, congenital birth defects and accidents.

206
00:17:02,480 --> 00:17:08,440
And in the days of the tall ships, the voyages of discovery and the great maritime battles,

207
00:17:08,441 --> 00:17:10,940
accidents were inevitable.

208
00:17:10,941 --> 00:17:14,440
A lot of the sea captains lost their arms

209
00:17:14,441 --> 00:17:16,440
when they were holding onto a rope,

210
00:17:16,441 --> 00:17:18,440
and the rope just pulled their hand off.

211
00:17:18,441 --> 00:17:20,440
And before you know it, they went around the ship,

212
00:17:20,441 --> 00:17:22,440
found a little hook and a little piece of leather,

213
00:17:22,441 --> 00:17:25,440
wrapped it around, and here they were the captain hook

214
00:17:25,441 --> 00:17:30,440
on the ship and back sailing and traveling the world once again.

215
00:17:30,441 --> 00:17:34,440
Despite the advances made in both surgical techniques

216
00:17:34,441 --> 00:17:37,440
and prosthetic devices over the centuries,

217
00:17:37,441 --> 00:17:41,380
these basic hooks and peg legs were destined to remain

218
00:17:41,480 --> 00:17:44,240
a popular option for many amputees

219
00:17:44,340 --> 00:17:46,680
until well into modern times.

220
00:17:46,780 --> 00:17:51,980
There's a catch on the back to allow need to be flexed,

221
00:17:52,080 --> 00:17:59,600
and it should lock in that position so that one can sit down.

222
00:17:59,700 --> 00:18:01,960
By the late 19th century, amputees

223
00:18:02,060 --> 00:18:07,440
could do a great deal more than just sit, stand, and walk.

224
00:18:07,441 --> 00:18:17,060
Of all the congenital birth defects requiring a prosthetic, the most common is the absence of the lower left arm.

225
00:18:17,061 --> 00:18:21,160
There is no satisfactory explanation for this phenomenon.

226
00:18:21,940 --> 00:18:25,500
Prosthetics will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

227
00:18:25,501 --> 00:18:31,840
Like the metal hooks and wooden pegs used by early sailors,

228
00:18:32,580 --> 00:18:35,140
many of the devices created during the Renaissance

229
00:18:35,141 --> 00:18:39,220
anticipated the designs of modern prosthetists.

230
00:18:40,140 --> 00:18:42,800
A Dutch surgeon called Verdun

231
00:18:42,801 --> 00:18:48,080
actually devised an artificial limb for a bologna amputee

232
00:18:48,081 --> 00:18:51,880
that had jointed side steels.

233
00:18:51,881 --> 00:18:54,120
They come up the side of the leg

234
00:18:54,121 --> 00:18:58,200
and then he had what he called a cuff,

235
00:18:58,820 --> 00:19:05,000
which is similar to what we have today and call a thigh corset.

236
00:19:05,960 --> 00:19:10,520
Not all advances in prosthetics came from doctors or surgeons.

237
00:19:11,420 --> 00:19:14,100
Throughout history, people in need of an artificial limb

238
00:19:14,101 --> 00:19:17,540
frequently turned to local craftspeople for assistance.

239
00:19:18,760 --> 00:19:20,820
Your blacksmith was your first prosthetist,

240
00:19:20,821 --> 00:19:24,080
and of course your carpenter was also a prosthetist,

241
00:19:24,120 --> 00:19:31,000
A carpenter could carve a peg leg, a carpenter could carve a wooden socket for a stump, for a leg to fit into.

242
00:19:31,800 --> 00:19:37,480
They would go to the person who was making saddles for horses and harnesses for horses.

243
00:19:38,200 --> 00:19:42,860
And the harness maker would turn around and build a prosthesis for this person.

244
00:19:43,380 --> 00:19:49,740
And I've seen prosthetic devices made out of ceramics, where the potter would actually make the socket.

245
00:19:49,741 --> 00:19:55,740
So there's been a lot of interventions from all forms of artistic people

246
00:19:55,741 --> 00:19:58,740
on how to get a person back up and walking again.

247
00:20:02,740 --> 00:20:07,740
By the early 1800s, a number of innovations had been made in artificial limbs,

248
00:20:07,741 --> 00:20:12,740
including the raviton leg, which consisted of a laced leather boot

249
00:20:12,741 --> 00:20:17,740
filled with horse hair and an ankle joint controlled by a spiral spring.

250
00:20:17,741 --> 00:20:22,860
A German dentist named Bailiff designed a hand with fingers

251
00:20:22,861 --> 00:20:26,480
that could be controlled by the movement of the remaining elbow joint,

252
00:20:26,840 --> 00:20:30,140
the first time that muscles in the residual limb

253
00:20:30,141 --> 00:20:33,180
were used to animate a mechanical device.

254
00:20:34,140 --> 00:20:37,360
He actually reversed what had been the standard procedure

255
00:20:37,361 --> 00:20:40,320
of passive opening and active closing.

256
00:20:40,840 --> 00:20:45,600
He had active opening of the hand and passive closing,

257
00:20:45,601 --> 00:20:48,320
which is what we actually have today.

258
00:20:48,940 --> 00:20:52,740
In 1800, an above-knee prosthesis

259
00:20:52,741 --> 00:20:54,740
consisting of two wooden cones

260
00:20:54,741 --> 00:20:57,040
with an articulated steel knee joint

261
00:20:57,041 --> 00:21:00,180
was patented by James Potts in England.

262
00:21:01,120 --> 00:21:04,960
This particular leg was different from all others

263
00:21:04,961 --> 00:21:10,900
because when the knee flexed, the foot also came up.

264
00:21:10,901 --> 00:21:15,900
It was linked by cords which ran through the shin.

265
00:21:16,900 --> 00:21:19,900
This enduring, ingenious prosthesis

266
00:21:19,901 --> 00:21:23,900
was known as the Anglesey leg after the Marquis of Anglesey,

267
00:21:23,901 --> 00:21:27,900
who used it after losing his own leg at the Battle of Waterloo.

268
00:21:27,901 --> 00:21:32,900
Until very, very recently, legs were being made

269
00:21:32,901 --> 00:21:37,900
on the Anglesey principle and still known as Anglesey legs.

270
00:21:37,901 --> 00:21:38,601
legs.

271
00:21:38,602 --> 00:21:47,260
In 1839, a former employee of James Potts, William Selfo, modified the Anglissi leg and

272
00:21:47,261 --> 00:21:49,187
brought it to the United States.

273
00:21:49,188 --> 00:21:52,860
Seven years after that, when an amputee named Benjamin Palmer

274
00:21:52,861 --> 00:21:59,020
altered the limb to suit his own needs and patented his improvements, the Anglissi leg

275
00:21:59,021 --> 00:22:01,528
became known as the American leg.

276
00:22:01,529 --> 00:22:05,860
It was an exciting time for a person who had lost a limb,

277
00:22:05,861 --> 00:22:09,200
where now when they would lose a limb, let's say above the knee,

278
00:22:10,080 --> 00:22:12,540
when they sit down, both knees would bend.

279
00:22:13,160 --> 00:22:18,600
When they stand up, they could walk with a knee flexing and extending just like the other side.

280
00:22:19,540 --> 00:22:25,040
The American leg was further modified in 1858 by a Dr. Douglas Bly,

281
00:22:25,620 --> 00:22:33,260
who introduced a movable ankle joint consisting of an ivory ball fitted into a socket of vulcanized rubber.

282
00:22:33,261 --> 00:22:40,760
Bly modestly referred to his leg as the most complete and successful invention ever attained

283
00:22:40,761 --> 00:22:47,080
in artificial limbs. Bly's leg and other improvements in the design of artificial

284
00:22:47,081 --> 00:22:53,120
limbs were influenced as ever by advances in surgical techniques, many of which were made

285
00:22:53,121 --> 00:23:00,100
possible by the French discovery in 1842 of the anesthetic properties of chloroform.

286
00:23:00,101 --> 00:23:06,780
JAMES SYME, With anesthesia, it began to be possible to produce a more satisfactory

287
00:23:06,781 --> 00:23:08,280
surgical result.

288
00:23:08,281 --> 00:23:14,480
James Syme, chief of surgery at the University of Glasgow, was actually the first

289
00:23:14,481 --> 00:23:20,320
surgeon to address the difficulties of creating a residual limb that could be easily fitted

290
00:23:20,321 --> 00:23:21,600
with a prosthesis.

291
00:23:21,601 --> 00:23:26,600
JAMES SYME, Chief of Surgery at the University of Glasgow, was actually the first surgeon

292
00:23:26,601 --> 00:23:29,600
to address the difficulties of creating a residual limb that could be easily fitted with a prosthesis.

293
00:23:29,601 --> 00:23:33,360
at the ankle joint, and he preserved the heel pad

294
00:23:33,361 --> 00:23:37,040
and sewed that into the residual limb

295
00:23:37,041 --> 00:23:39,880
and created a soft pad at the end

296
00:23:39,881 --> 00:23:43,280
that would allow the tissues to be protected

297
00:23:43,281 --> 00:23:46,220
by a block of shock-absorbing material

298
00:23:46,221 --> 00:23:50,560
that is really quite close to what nature originally intended.

299
00:23:50,561 --> 00:23:55,800
In 1867, Syme's student and son-in-law Joseph Lister

300
00:23:55,801 --> 00:23:59,180
made a major contribution to medicine and prosthetics

301
00:23:59,181 --> 00:24:02,880
with the publication of Principles of Antiseptic Surgery.

302
00:24:03,760 --> 00:24:06,660
Unfortunately, this landmark work came too late

303
00:24:06,661 --> 00:24:09,700
to help the victims of America's bloody civil war,

304
00:24:10,160 --> 00:24:14,640
where 30,000 amputations were performed just by the Union Army surgeons.

305
00:24:16,380 --> 00:24:19,380
They wore the bloody aprons, they didn't wear gloves,

306
00:24:19,740 --> 00:24:21,720
they didn't wash their hands, so they cut off this limb,

307
00:24:21,780 --> 00:24:24,740
they go to the next person, cut off that limb, the next person, the next person.

308
00:24:24,741 --> 00:24:29,180
So the germs were rampant throughout the battlefield.

309
00:24:30,500 --> 00:24:36,000
Despite the septic conditions, thousands of amputees survived the Civil War,

310
00:24:36,520 --> 00:24:40,020
creating an unprecedented demand for artificial limbs.

311
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:42,403
In 1861, James E.

312
00:24:42,404 --> 00:24:48,840
Hanger, a Confederate soldier who lost his leg in the first battle of the war,

313
00:24:48,841 --> 00:24:52,780
recognized this need and founded America's first

314
00:24:52,781 --> 00:24:56,520
and now its largest prosthetics manufacturing company.

315
00:24:57,200 --> 00:25:01,180
He decided that he could make a lighter weight prosthesis

316
00:25:01,181 --> 00:25:04,320
and came up with some of the first metal limbs

317
00:25:04,321 --> 00:25:06,280
back over 100 years ago.

318
00:25:06,880 --> 00:25:11,500
And those metal limbs today continue to be fit in England,

319
00:25:11,760 --> 00:25:14,120
which is incredible, you know,

320
00:25:14,240 --> 00:25:17,380
that 100-year-old egg is still fit today.

321
00:25:17,381 --> 00:25:20,480
He had his own kind of foot, special articulating foot,

322
00:25:20,580 --> 00:25:24,380
and his own type of knee, a hanger knee, a hanger foot,

323
00:25:24,480 --> 00:25:28,400
a hanger strap with his signature on it.

324
00:25:28,500 --> 00:25:30,260
The Civil War marked the beginning

325
00:25:30,360 --> 00:25:33,860
of a remarkable period in American prosthetics history.

326
00:25:33,960 --> 00:25:37,900
Over the next 50 years, nearly 250 patents

327
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:39,940
were filed for such diverse inventions

328
00:25:40,040 --> 00:25:44,100
as rubber feet, suction sockets, ankle joints,

329
00:25:44,200 --> 00:25:47,380
and articulated limbs.

330
00:25:47,381 --> 00:25:50,620
Continually today, people apply for patents

331
00:25:50,621 --> 00:25:54,360
and to find that, wow, somebody came up with this 50 years ago

332
00:25:54,361 --> 00:25:56,380
or 100 years ago.

333
00:25:56,381 --> 00:25:59,000
This revolution in prosthetics design

334
00:25:59,001 --> 00:26:01,760
continued into the early 20th century,

335
00:26:01,761 --> 00:26:05,300
with such remarkable advances as Marcel de Souter's all

336
00:26:05,301 --> 00:26:09,800
aluminum leg and the split hook developed by D.W. Dorrance,

337
00:26:09,801 --> 00:26:12,740
a version of which is still being used today.

338
00:26:12,741 --> 00:26:16,900
And there's not been many advantages or advances

339
00:26:16,901 --> 00:26:20,680
since then, except this hook is now made out of aluminum.

340
00:26:20,681 --> 00:26:23,680
My first hook was steel.

341
00:26:23,681 --> 00:26:26,960
Individuals continue to design prosthetics,

342
00:26:26,961 --> 00:26:30,420
but the actual business of manufacturing artificial limbs

343
00:26:30,421 --> 00:26:33,580
was dominated by companies like J.E. Hanger

344
00:26:33,581 --> 00:26:36,980
and A.A. Marks of New York.

345
00:26:36,981 --> 00:26:38,940
Amputees could also get their limbs

346
00:26:38,941 --> 00:26:41,960
direct from the pages of such mail order catalogs

347
00:26:41,961 --> 00:26:45,240
as Sears and Roebuck or Montgomery Wards.

348
00:26:45,241 --> 00:26:47,620
But the greatest advances in the art and science

349
00:26:47,621 --> 00:26:51,540
of prosthetics were inspired once again by war,

350
00:26:51,541 --> 00:26:53,160
the First World War.

351
00:26:56,240 --> 00:26:58,960
Germany's Kaiser Wilhelm II was born

352
00:26:58,961 --> 00:27:00,920
with a withered left arm.

353
00:27:00,921 --> 00:27:04,500
Only his closest advisors knew that this paralyzed limb

354
00:27:04,501 --> 00:27:07,140
was strapped to his body so that he could wear

355
00:27:07,141 --> 00:27:10,000
a prosthetic arm attached to his shoulder.

356
00:27:10,940 --> 00:27:14,740
Prosthetics will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

357
00:27:15,240 --> 00:27:24,340
With the onset of World War I, the focus on prosthetic design and manufacturing shifted back to Europe.

358
00:27:25,300 --> 00:27:32,000
The American expeditionary forces suffered 4,403 amputations in the war.

359
00:27:34,940 --> 00:27:42,480
Injuries resulting in amputation in all the armies of Europe, however, totaled a staggering 300,000.

360
00:27:42,481 --> 00:27:46,360
most of them the result of brutal fighting in the trenches.

361
00:27:47,140 --> 00:27:49,800
There were no antibiotics then,

362
00:27:50,200 --> 00:27:55,620
and it was found that infection occurred very, very quickly.

363
00:27:56,660 --> 00:28:00,340
And if a wound was stitched up with the infection inside,

364
00:28:00,800 --> 00:28:02,160
it was ten times worse,

365
00:28:02,760 --> 00:28:08,080
and the patient would die of what is known as gas gangrene.

366
00:28:08,081 --> 00:28:13,480
To prevent this happening, immediate amputation was performed,

367
00:28:14,260 --> 00:28:18,460
and that's why there were so many thousands of amputations

368
00:28:18,461 --> 00:28:22,780
performed during the First World War, far more than in the Second War.

369
00:28:24,020 --> 00:28:27,960
In 1915, the British responded to this crisis

370
00:28:27,961 --> 00:28:33,560
by establishing a pioneering research and rehabilitation center at Roehampton,

371
00:28:34,020 --> 00:28:36,200
a country mansion outside London.

372
00:28:37,200 --> 00:28:40,520
Queen Mary's Hospital was also founded there,

373
00:28:40,820 --> 00:28:44,840
and an entire prosthetics industry grew up around Roehampton.

374
00:28:46,120 --> 00:28:48,300
Thanks in part to the work done there,

375
00:28:48,820 --> 00:28:53,200
most wartime amputees were restored to some measure of function.

376
00:28:54,660 --> 00:28:59,680
For the first time, surgeons routinely consulted with prosthetists

377
00:28:59,681 --> 00:29:04,200
to determine the most effective way to shape the residual limb

378
00:29:04,201 --> 00:29:09,200
in order to achieve a comfortable fit with the artificial device.

379
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:14,740
The level of amputation has a lot to do with your success in rehabilitation.

380
00:29:15,340 --> 00:29:19,560
The higher the amputation, the more difficult it is to fit with a prosthetic device,

381
00:29:19,640 --> 00:29:21,180
and the less functional it is.

382
00:29:22,180 --> 00:29:25,660
Fitting the patient almost immediately after surgery

383
00:29:25,661 --> 00:29:29,260
also improved his chances of recovering function.

384
00:29:29,261 --> 00:29:34,640
This is a temporary prosthesis for a below-knee amputation.

385
00:29:35,780 --> 00:29:38,000
It's known as a fill-pot limb,

386
00:29:38,740 --> 00:29:42,600
and it was fibre with a comfortably,

387
00:29:42,860 --> 00:29:45,640
or hopefully comfortably padded socket.

388
00:29:46,120 --> 00:29:50,140
This was only a temporary limb while a proper limb was being made

389
00:29:50,141 --> 00:29:56,680
and also while the stump was settling down after the amputation.

390
00:29:56,681 --> 00:30:04,880
Once again, many of the prosthetic designs that emerged from the war came from the amputees themselves.

391
00:30:06,160 --> 00:30:09,520
This was designed by a major light foot.

392
00:30:10,520 --> 00:30:18,220
Now these were given, like the Philpott leg, as a temporary limb, while a proper limb was going to be made for them.

393
00:30:18,221 --> 00:30:29,740
Despite the number of veterans needing prostheses and the development of such potentially useful

394
00:30:29,741 --> 00:30:36,540
new materials as nylon and bakelite, there was still no centralized effort to improve

395
00:30:36,541 --> 00:30:40,230
artificial limbs, either in Europe or the U.S.

396
00:30:40,231 --> 00:30:43,680
And so in the years between the World Wars,

397
00:30:43,681 --> 00:30:48,480
many patients preferred to stick with familiar time-tested materials,

398
00:30:48,760 --> 00:30:53,880
and wooden sockets were still painstakingly pulled and shaped by hand

399
00:30:53,881 --> 00:30:56,840
just as they'd been 100 years earlier.

400
00:30:57,760 --> 00:31:00,480
You take this tool and you can relieve this.

401
00:31:01,820 --> 00:31:02,520
So that's so.

402
00:31:03,000 --> 00:31:06,060
Then after you get it dug out so as far as you think you might need it,

403
00:31:06,080 --> 00:31:07,120
then you have to hand sand it.

404
00:31:07,440 --> 00:31:09,420
You have to go back to the patient again and try him again.

405
00:31:10,060 --> 00:31:12,320
This leg comes from about the 1920s,

406
00:31:12,321 --> 00:31:15,760
and it's practically all wood.

407
00:31:16,760 --> 00:31:18,680
And in order to give the wood back then,

408
00:31:19,240 --> 00:31:23,120
they put leather all around the wood.

409
00:31:23,740 --> 00:31:28,080
And what happens is they steep the leather in water

410
00:31:28,081 --> 00:31:31,300
and bring it up and wrap it around the socket here

411
00:31:31,301 --> 00:31:35,740
and around the shin section and let it dry overnight

412
00:31:35,741 --> 00:31:37,400
and it just tightens in around the wood

413
00:31:37,401 --> 00:31:39,120
and keeps it very solid and strong.

414
00:31:39,121 --> 00:31:43,560
The demand for prosthetic legs is usually far less

415
00:31:43,660 --> 00:31:45,600
than it is for upper limbs.

416
00:31:45,700 --> 00:31:49,860
But the 1920s and 30s still saw some remarkable individual

417
00:31:49,960 --> 00:31:53,740
efforts to take on the greatest prosthetic challenge of all,

418
00:31:53,840 --> 00:31:57,700
to create hands that combined a pleasing natural appearance

419
00:31:57,800 --> 00:32:00,780
with a significant degree of function.

420
00:32:00,880 --> 00:32:06,180
When a hand is missing, it is of vital importance

421
00:32:06,181 --> 00:32:13,420
Once that function is maintained, and as far as the patient is concerned, cosmesis is retained.

422
00:32:13,940 --> 00:32:16,100
But it is very difficult to have the two.

423
00:32:16,920 --> 00:32:23,200
The advantage of this hand is that when the person picks something up, all fingers move.

424
00:32:24,060 --> 00:32:26,360
And how they operate is through a cable.

425
00:32:27,040 --> 00:32:33,200
And the cable goes right off through a little control unit here where they can actually lock the fingers closed.

426
00:32:33,201 --> 00:32:34,980
And the person will do that with their sound hand.

427
00:32:34,981 --> 00:32:39,700
It's a fascinating piece of equipment, just beautifully engineered.

428
00:32:44,740 --> 00:32:51,000
It was war once again that brought about the next major changes in prosthetics.

429
00:32:53,580 --> 00:32:58,700
In 1945, as the number of amputees in World War II mounted,

430
00:32:59,540 --> 00:33:04,100
Surgeon General of the Army Norman Kirk requested that the National Academy of Science

431
00:33:04,101 --> 00:33:08,460
convene a committee of surgeons, engineers, and prosthetists

432
00:33:08,461 --> 00:33:12,580
to investigate the best ways in which the most effective limbs

433
00:33:12,581 --> 00:33:14,960
could be provided to disabled veterans.

434
00:33:16,240 --> 00:33:20,120
The National Academy of Science, all those high-level people,

435
00:33:20,520 --> 00:33:22,520
figured out what veterans already knew,

436
00:33:22,660 --> 00:33:24,320
and that was the quality of limbs was poor,

437
00:33:24,400 --> 00:33:25,460
and there was no way of delivering them.

438
00:33:27,020 --> 00:33:30,160
Out of that came this startling fact

439
00:33:30,161 --> 00:33:39,140
that there had been no scientific approach to the amputations and processes.

440
00:33:39,940 --> 00:33:43,640
And so they said, well, we've got to do something about this.

441
00:33:43,641 --> 00:33:56,000
The idea was that the surgeons would give the engineers the criteria needed to design processes to satisfy the needs of the amputees.

442
00:33:56,001 --> 00:34:04,380
but it turned out that the surgeons didn't have the fundamental data to give on locomotion

443
00:34:04,381 --> 00:34:11,520
and the functions of the upper limb in order to get design criteria.

444
00:34:12,660 --> 00:34:18,020
The NAS established laboratories at the University of California, Berkeley

445
00:34:18,021 --> 00:34:21,960
to do lower extremity, gait, and locomotion studies,

446
00:34:21,961 --> 00:34:25,740
which resulted in the solid ankle cushion heel, or sack foot,

447
00:34:26,000 --> 00:34:31,680
UCLA, meanwhile, concentrated on researching upper limb biomechanics.

448
00:34:32,680 --> 00:34:37,900
We couldn't quite wait for all those studies to be finished

449
00:34:37,901 --> 00:34:41,680
before it was necessary to start gadgeteering.

450
00:34:42,040 --> 00:34:46,740
When we kind of got the information and were able to apply it,

451
00:34:46,800 --> 00:34:51,660
then the gadgeteering became scientific.

452
00:34:51,661 --> 00:34:56,660
Industry, particularly the aviation industry,

453
00:34:56,760 --> 00:34:59,360
also contributed to the development of lighter,

454
00:34:59,460 --> 00:35:03,200
more functional prosthetics in the post-war years.

455
00:35:03,300 --> 00:35:05,400
Plastics were an integral part of the development

456
00:35:05,500 --> 00:35:06,980
of many airplanes.

457
00:35:07,080 --> 00:35:09,720
And the lamination process, lamination

458
00:35:09,820 --> 00:35:13,720
was something that flight aircraft engineers knew

459
00:35:13,820 --> 00:35:16,720
was lightweight, it was layered, and it was structurally

460
00:35:16,820 --> 00:35:20,420
stronger than just a solid piece of metal or wood.

461
00:35:20,421 --> 00:35:29,100
And what you always strive for with any device is to make it lighter and to make it easy to work,

462
00:35:29,380 --> 00:35:33,920
because the body, it takes a lot of energy to use an artificial limb.

463
00:35:35,500 --> 00:35:43,500
In the 1960s, the use of the drug thalidomide by pregnant women resulted in thousands of horrific birth defects

464
00:35:43,501 --> 00:35:46,760
and prompted intensive prosthetics research.

465
00:35:46,920 --> 00:35:50,300
These were the prostheses worn by one of my young patients.

466
00:35:50,420 --> 00:35:52,680
and one foot would not go down.

467
00:35:52,780 --> 00:35:54,300
It was in this position.

468
00:35:54,400 --> 00:35:56,960
And that is the position in which this plate is set.

469
00:35:59,600 --> 00:36:02,460
But the most dramatic advances in artificial limbs

470
00:36:02,560 --> 00:36:03,700
were yet to come.

471
00:36:09,000 --> 00:36:12,740
Today, the most common cause of lower limb amputation

472
00:36:12,840 --> 00:36:17,020
is smoking, which accelerates hardening of the arteries

473
00:36:17,021 --> 00:36:20,580
and often results in the loss of both legs.

474
00:36:21,840 --> 00:36:24,920
Prosthetics will return on Modern Marvels.

475
00:36:27,500 --> 00:36:32,080
The extensive research into biomechanics and engineering

476
00:36:32,081 --> 00:36:36,980
conducted by scientists, engineers, and prosthetists after World War II

477
00:36:36,981 --> 00:36:39,760
resulted in one definite conclusion.

478
00:36:40,680 --> 00:36:45,840
The socket, the oldest and most basic component in any artificial limb,

479
00:36:45,841 --> 00:36:48,280
was also the most important.

480
00:36:49,520 --> 00:36:51,900
The socket's always been the most important part

481
00:36:51,901 --> 00:36:53,600
of any prosthesis,

482
00:36:53,680 --> 00:36:55,180
because that's where the rubber meets the road.

483
00:36:55,600 --> 00:36:58,340
You've got to have a good-fitting socket

484
00:36:58,341 --> 00:36:59,240
where it's just miserable.

485
00:36:59,360 --> 00:37:00,940
It's like if you get a little pebble in your shoe,

486
00:37:01,280 --> 00:37:02,240
it just makes you miserable.

487
00:37:03,320 --> 00:37:04,300
The commission,

488
00:37:04,600 --> 00:37:07,420
formed by Surgeon General of the Army Norman Kirk,

489
00:37:08,020 --> 00:37:11,080
suggested a re-examination of the suction socket,

490
00:37:11,081 --> 00:37:16,180
first introduced by Dubois de Parmelie in 1868.

491
00:37:16,740 --> 00:37:21,020
So scientists began experimenting with various socket shapes

492
00:37:21,021 --> 00:37:23,040
made out of hard plastics.

493
00:37:24,360 --> 00:37:29,220
These high-tech plastics were very rigid and solid.

494
00:37:29,420 --> 00:37:32,540
So when you put the residual limb into one of these solid sockets,

495
00:37:33,260 --> 00:37:36,820
due to the lack of movement and the lack of forgiveness in the socket,

496
00:37:37,620 --> 00:37:39,840
the muscle would atrophy inside that socket

497
00:37:39,841 --> 00:37:41,380
and it would have this suction on.

498
00:37:41,880 --> 00:37:47,460
Because the suction is there, the patient wouldn't have the perceived need

499
00:37:47,461 --> 00:37:50,120
to fire up the muscle to hold the socket in place.

500
00:37:50,980 --> 00:37:54,660
This socket shape is the quadrilateral socket shape,

501
00:37:54,880 --> 00:37:58,680
which has quadril means four, one, two, three, four sides.

502
00:37:59,140 --> 00:38:03,680
And they tried to, they thought it would turn less on the body if they made it square.

503
00:38:04,400 --> 00:38:06,360
But it didn't turn out to be that way really

504
00:38:06,361 --> 00:38:09,900
because you only have one femur bone going down inside here,

505
00:38:10,060 --> 00:38:11,580
and like a knife in a jelly jar,

506
00:38:11,700 --> 00:38:13,740
you can still turn that jelly jar around the knife.

507
00:38:14,660 --> 00:38:19,320
John Sabolich solved many of these problems in the 1980s

508
00:38:19,321 --> 00:38:21,620
with his patented Sabolich socket,

509
00:38:22,240 --> 00:38:24,680
the result of years of cadaver studies.

510
00:38:25,480 --> 00:38:27,520
A soft, flexible, fitted socket,

511
00:38:27,900 --> 00:38:30,340
it's supported by a carbon fiber frame.

512
00:38:32,360 --> 00:38:34,520
Utilize the hard plastic on the outside of it,

513
00:38:34,521 --> 00:38:38,200
utilize the flexible plastic on the inside.

514
00:38:40,760 --> 00:38:44,120
Unlike hard sockets, the flexible sockets surrounds

515
00:38:44,121 --> 00:38:47,860
and supports every bone and muscle in the residual limb,

516
00:38:48,320 --> 00:38:52,380
thereby providing the wearer with an unprecedented level of comfort

517
00:38:52,381 --> 00:38:53,580
and range of motion.

518
00:38:54,840 --> 00:38:58,520
You can have all the fancy componentry, all the hydraulics

519
00:38:58,521 --> 00:39:01,760
and pneumatics and electronics and sense of feel and all that stuff.

520
00:39:01,761 --> 00:39:06,400
the prosthesis would wind up in the closet if the socket doesn't fit.

521
00:39:06,780 --> 00:39:07,560
How does it feel?

522
00:39:07,860 --> 00:39:08,560
It feels great.

523
00:39:09,060 --> 00:39:12,520
The dramatically improved function of modern prosthetics

524
00:39:12,521 --> 00:39:16,800
is matched only by the equally stunning improvements in cosmesis

525
00:39:16,801 --> 00:39:19,520
or appearance of the artificial limbs.

526
00:39:20,580 --> 00:39:24,060
This is a silicone restoration foot.

527
00:39:24,061 --> 00:39:29,640
It's completely bendable and pliable and very realistic looking,

528
00:39:29,641 --> 00:39:35,080
looking at the ridges in the foot and the heel and the bottom of the foot.

529
00:39:37,120 --> 00:39:41,440
You can see veins and hair, even muscle tone in the prosthesis.

530
00:39:43,000 --> 00:39:47,900
Unfortunately, it's not yet possible to combine the latest cosmetic advances

531
00:39:47,901 --> 00:39:51,040
with the highest level of function in the same limb.

532
00:39:52,060 --> 00:39:55,660
Silicon restoration, very aesthetically pleasing, but a lot less durable.

533
00:39:55,661 --> 00:40:01,240
very susceptible to ribs, tears, newspaper ink, ballpoint pen.

534
00:40:06,220 --> 00:40:09,480
Amputees can now customize their prostheses,

535
00:40:09,800 --> 00:40:14,200
choosing from among a vast range of both functional and cosmetic components.

536
00:40:15,320 --> 00:40:19,420
Sophisticated myoelectric elbows, wrists, and hands,

537
00:40:19,920 --> 00:40:23,400
which use electrical impulses from the residual limb

538
00:40:23,401 --> 00:40:26,700
to stimulate movement in the artificial device

539
00:40:26,701 --> 00:40:30,580
are now readily available to upper extremity amputees.

540
00:40:30,940 --> 00:40:33,260
When the muscle triggers a signal,

541
00:40:33,820 --> 00:40:35,820
we can pick that up on the surface of the skin

542
00:40:35,821 --> 00:40:37,920
with a small electrode that's non-invasive

543
00:40:37,921 --> 00:40:41,120
so that when the muscle fires, we read it from an electrode.

544
00:40:41,300 --> 00:40:43,580
The electrode tells the computer, hey, this muscle's firing,

545
00:40:43,700 --> 00:40:45,600
and then it operates a switch to either open the hand

546
00:40:45,601 --> 00:40:47,720
or close the hand or rotate the wrist

547
00:40:47,721 --> 00:40:48,800
or raise or lower the elbow.

548
00:40:50,240 --> 00:40:53,300
Lower extremity amputees have even more options

549
00:40:53,301 --> 00:40:57,260
including hydraulic knees and energy storing feet

550
00:40:57,261 --> 00:41:00,320
that allow even bilateral amputees

551
00:41:00,321 --> 00:41:02,940
to participate in the fiercest competitions.

552
00:41:04,320 --> 00:41:06,460
Here's a prosthesis for the cutaway design

553
00:41:06,461 --> 00:41:09,360
showing the flex foot with the toe lever and the heel lever.

554
00:41:09,800 --> 00:41:12,660
So as the patient walks, the heel lever compresses,

555
00:41:12,920 --> 00:41:14,220
rolling forward the foot flat.

556
00:41:14,640 --> 00:41:17,460
As they come across the toe, the toe lever compresses

557
00:41:17,461 --> 00:41:18,840
and shooting them forward.

558
00:41:18,841 --> 00:41:26,600
The same technology is carried through to running prostheses, which have only a tow lever.

559
00:41:26,860 --> 00:41:30,680
So as they sprint or run on the prostheses, the impact is taken here.

560
00:41:31,200 --> 00:41:35,740
The energy is absorbed into the curvature of the carbon fiber spring,

561
00:41:36,360 --> 00:41:41,280
stored energy like a leaf spring on a pickup truck, and then propels them forward at an expounded rate.

562
00:41:41,540 --> 00:41:42,280
Can't believe it.

563
00:41:42,281 --> 00:41:45,620
In recent years, researchers have also been developing

564
00:41:45,720 --> 00:41:48,380
sensory devices for artificial limbs.

565
00:41:48,480 --> 00:41:49,820
It's starting to get warm.

566
00:41:49,920 --> 00:41:52,620
We've done a good job of the motion, the mechanics.

567
00:41:52,720 --> 00:41:55,020
We've done a very poor job of the whole other half

568
00:41:55,120 --> 00:41:57,320
of the central nervous system.

569
00:41:57,420 --> 00:42:01,340
Hot and cold systems let an amputee differentiate

570
00:42:01,440 --> 00:42:03,740
between hot and cold.

571
00:42:03,840 --> 00:42:06,840
A temperature sensor in the artificial hand attached

572
00:42:06,940 --> 00:42:10,140
to a microprocessor in the artificial arm

573
00:42:10,141 --> 00:42:15,020
arm either warms or cools two thermal piles touching

574
00:42:15,120 --> 00:42:16,140
the residual limb.

575
00:42:22,660 --> 00:42:25,320
The sense of feel allows amputees

576
00:42:25,420 --> 00:42:28,220
to feel where they're placing their feet

577
00:42:28,320 --> 00:42:31,760
or how hard they're squeezing their hands.

578
00:42:31,860 --> 00:42:34,700
The harder you press, the more feeling you get back

579
00:42:34,800 --> 00:42:38,060
in terms of either vibration or electro stimulation, which

580
00:42:38,061 --> 00:42:40,340
which is a tingly, thinly.

581
00:42:40,341 --> 00:42:43,500
Our ultimate goal is to provide all the sensations

582
00:42:43,501 --> 00:42:46,440
that you or I have to the amputee.

583
00:42:46,441 --> 00:42:50,500
But not every amputee is eager to try these complicated

584
00:42:50,501 --> 00:42:52,820
and expensive devices.

585
00:42:52,821 --> 00:42:55,040
For many people like Fred Downs, who still

586
00:42:55,041 --> 00:42:59,420
uses a variation of the Dorrance Hook developed in 1912,

587
00:42:59,421 --> 00:43:02,900
new is not necessarily better.

588
00:43:02,901 --> 00:43:07,180
Terminator, Star Wars, robotics,

589
00:43:07,181 --> 00:43:10,760
That's what people's expectations are of how well these things work.

590
00:43:11,400 --> 00:43:17,000
And of course, Hollywood has built everybody up to expect these things to work as well as they do,

591
00:43:17,001 --> 00:43:23,440
not recognizing in reality batteries go dead, electrical things come unplugged,

592
00:43:23,500 --> 00:43:26,280
servo mechanisms break down and are heavy.

593
00:43:26,940 --> 00:43:30,800
Still, with today's rapidly evolving technology,

594
00:43:30,801 --> 00:43:37,660
the future of prosthetics is limited only by cost and the imagination of its practitioners.

595
00:43:38,580 --> 00:43:44,240
The researchers, engineers, surgeons, and scientists who are striving to create artificial limbs

596
00:43:44,241 --> 00:43:48,900
that will look and function almost as well as the real thing.

597
00:43:49,680 --> 00:43:55,700
But the ultimate success of all these futuristic devices is still dependent

598
00:43:55,701 --> 00:44:00,540
upon the skill and the art of the prosthetist.

599
00:44:00,800 --> 00:44:12,680
It takes a real artist to fit one of these devices to the human body.

600
00:44:13,280 --> 00:44:14,500
Now, you have a lot of mechanics.

601
00:44:14,800 --> 00:44:15,780
They do it by the numbers.

602
00:44:16,480 --> 00:44:21,660
But it takes an artist to look at the device and then meld it into the human body, onto

603
00:44:21,661 --> 00:44:26,160
the human body, so that it then becomes functional and it's comfortable and it feels...

